1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,122
Episode 15 ended on a
picture we never explained.

2
00:00:03,162 --> 00:00:08,030
Two hundred providers on the funnel,
and a quarter of them outside it.

3
00:00:08,167 --> 00:00:12,309
When the providers were identical,
about one in twenty fell outside.

4
00:00:12,349 --> 00:00:15,828
That was luck, and we knew
how much of it to expect.

5
00:00:15,868 --> 00:00:19,760
A quarter is something else.
Luck does not stretch that far.

6
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:24,404
So is a quarter of the crowd outliers?
Or is something wrong with the test?

7
00:00:24,444 --> 00:00:28,975
Neither. The test is answering a
question that has stopped being useful.

8
00:00:30,567 --> 00:00:32,788
Welcome back to Model in Motion.

9
00:00:32,828 --> 00:00:36,080
Today, the last episode: wider than chance.

10
00:00:38,167 --> 00:00:41,803
Our crowd has a knob: how
much providers truly differ.

11
00:00:41,843 --> 00:00:45,935
Set it to zero, and every
ordinary provider is the same.

12
00:00:45,975 --> 00:00:51,939
Give each one its z: its distance from the
reference, in units of what luck allows.

13
00:00:51,979 --> 00:00:57,248
The pile of them hugs the bell
curve, and 11 of the 194 are flagged.

14
00:00:57,367 --> 00:00:59,674
Now turn the knob, in three steps.

15
00:00:59,714 --> 00:01:05,397
The pile spreads. And the flags
climb: 16, then 35, then 48.

16
00:01:05,533 --> 00:01:11,344
At the last setting, a typical provider's
odds differ from the reference by roughly 30%.

17
00:01:11,384 --> 00:01:15,590
That is not much. But it is
real, and the test can see it.

18
00:01:17,167 --> 00:01:23,153
Look at who is flagged. Of the providers
with 200 patients or more, 40%.

19
00:01:23,193 --> 00:01:26,221
Of those with fewer than 50, 7%.

20
00:01:26,367 --> 00:01:30,355
The reason fits in one line. A
provider's z spreads by chance,

21
00:01:30,395 --> 00:01:33,539
and also by its true
distance from the reference,

22
00:01:33,579 --> 00:01:36,588
measured against how precisely we know it.

23
00:01:36,628 --> 00:01:41,265
For a provider known to within 0.1,
that second part swamps the first.

24
00:01:41,305 --> 00:01:45,408
Its z has three times the spread
that chance alone would give,

25
00:01:45,448 --> 00:01:48,605
and it is flagged more than half the time.

26
00:01:48,733 --> 00:01:50,489
Large providers are not worse.

27
00:01:50,529 --> 00:01:54,651
They are measured better, so
their small true differences show.

28
00:01:56,233 --> 00:02:00,431
So nothing is wrong with the test.
Every one of those flags is true:

29
00:02:00,471 --> 00:02:03,617
that provider is not exactly at the reference.

30
00:02:03,657 --> 00:02:08,935
But with enough patients, nobody is. The
question has stopped sorting providers.

31
00:02:09,067 --> 00:02:13,938
Here is a better one. Not: is this
provider exactly at the reference?

32
00:02:13,978 --> 00:02:17,673
But: is this provider
unusual, among providers?

33
00:02:19,267 --> 00:02:22,676
To answer that, we stop taking
the bell curve on faith,

34
00:02:22,716 --> 00:02:27,952
and fit one to the providers themselves:
to the central bulk of their z-values.

35
00:02:27,992 --> 00:02:30,875
That fitted curve is called an empirical null.

36
00:02:30,915 --> 00:02:36,253
We do it separately for small,
medium and large providers.

37
00:02:36,293 --> 00:02:40,630
The fitted spreads are 1.14, 1.60 and 2.18.

38
00:02:40,767 --> 00:02:47,255
Judge each provider against its own group's
curve, and 13 are flagged, in place of 52.

39
00:02:47,295 --> 00:02:50,101
Across twenty redraws of the crowd,

40
00:02:50,141 --> 00:02:55,453
the share of ordinary providers
flagged falls from 23% to 5%.

41
00:02:55,600 --> 00:03:02,246
On the funnel, it is a wider pair
of limits for each size group.

42
00:03:02,286 --> 00:03:11,203
For a provider of 280 patients, they move
from 0.76 and 1.24 out to 0.50 and 1.57.

43
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:15,708
It is not free. Of our six planted providers,

44
00:03:15,748 --> 00:03:19,635
the standard test caught
four. This one catches three.

45
00:03:19,767 --> 00:03:24,743
The curve is itself an estimate, and it
can miss. With the knob back at zero,

46
00:03:24,783 --> 00:03:27,349
this crowd's curves came out too narrow,

47
00:03:27,389 --> 00:03:30,927
and flagged 20 where the
standard test flagged 11.

48
00:03:30,967 --> 00:03:34,020
And it assumes that most
providers are ordinary.

49
00:03:34,060 --> 00:03:37,440
If many are not, the
curve widens to hide them.

50
00:03:37,567 --> 00:03:40,747
Above all, it has not
made anything more exact.

51
00:03:40,787 --> 00:03:45,932
It has changed the question. A flag
now means: unusual among providers.

52
00:03:45,972 --> 00:03:47,872
There are other answers too:

53
00:03:47,912 --> 00:03:53,320
the shrunken estimates of Episode 17,
or no flags at all, only intervals.

54
00:03:54,900 --> 00:03:56,396
Let's gather it up.

55
00:03:56,436 --> 00:03:58,730
When providers truly differ a little,

56
00:03:58,770 --> 00:04:04,097
their test statistics spread wider than
chance, most of all for large providers.

57
00:04:04,137 --> 00:04:08,702
The standard test asks whether a
provider is exactly at the reference.

58
00:04:08,742 --> 00:04:13,100
The empirical null asks whether
it is unusual among providers.

59
00:04:13,140 --> 00:04:16,626
Choosing between them is
choosing what a flag should mean.

60
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:21,055
And that is the logistic fixed-effect model,

61
00:04:21,095 --> 00:04:24,731
from an unfair comparison
to a flag you can defend.

62
00:04:24,771 --> 00:04:30,131
One intercept for every provider. A fit
that balances observed against expected.

63
00:04:30,171 --> 00:04:35,261
A ratio that asks what the reference
would have done with the same patients.

64
00:04:35,301 --> 00:04:38,689
And a test that knows how far luck can reach.

65
00:04:39,467 --> 00:04:43,572
This has been Model in Motion.
Thanks for following the story.
